{"feedback": "Hypothesis: Among deceased patients, higher tumor mutational burden (TMB) is associated with longer overall survival (days_to_death), suggesting a neoantigen-driven benefit.

Implementation: The clinical and mutation datasets were loaded; deceased patients with non-missing days_to_death (n=104) were selected; TMB (mutation counts) was computed; datasets were merged. A Spearman correlation yielded rho = –0.191 (p = 0.0527), indicating a weak negative trend (higher TMB linked to shorter survival) that narrowly misses significance. An optional Cox model (using only event times for deceased) produced HR per mutation = 1.001 (95% CI 0.999–1.003, p = 0.275), showing no protective effect of TMB. A scatterplot with smoothing line visualizes the slight negative association and substantial variability.

Findings: No evidence supports the hypothesis that higher TMB predicts longer survival among deceased patients; if anything, a marginal negative trend was observed. Further analysis with proper censoring in a full-cohort Cox model may refine these insights.", "success": true}

{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Triple‐negative breast cancer patients (ER−/PR−/HER2−) exhibit higher expression of immune checkpoint genes (e.g., PDCD1, CD274, CTLA4) than hormone receptor‐positive subtypes.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare immune checkpoint gene expression between triple‐negative and non‐triple‐negative breast cancer patients.",  
      "steps":"1. Load clinical and gene expression data; 2. Define subgroups: triple‐negative (er_status='Negative', pr_status='Negative', her2_ihc_status='Negative') versus others; 3. Extract expression values for immune checkpoint genes: PDCD1 (PD‐1), CD274 (PD‐L1), CTLA4; 4. Merge subgroup labels with expression matrix by patient barcode; 5. For each gene, compare expression distributions between groups using Wilcoxon rank‐sum tests; 6. Adjust p‐values for multiple testing (e.g., Benjamini–Hochberg); 7. Generate boxplots of gene expression by subgroup.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of median expression and IQR for each checkpoint gene by subgroup; 2. Wilcoxon test statistics, raw and adjusted p‐values; 3. Boxplots for each gene showing subgroup differences."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Higher expression of the proliferation marker MKI67 is positively associated with larger tumor size (pathologic T stage T2/T3 vs T1), independent of estrogen receptor status.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether MKI67 expression varies by pathologic tumor size (T stage) after adjusting for ER status.",  
      "steps":"1. Load clinical and gene expression data; 2. Extract pathologic_t and er_status; group T stage into two categories: T1 vs T2_or_higher (T2/T3/T4); 3. Extract MKI67 expression per patient; 4. Build a linear regression model: MKI67_expression ~ T_stage_group + ER_status; 5. Check model assumptions (residuals normality, homoscedasticity); if violated, use non‐parametric approach (stratified Wilcoxon); 6. Report coefficient for T_stage_group and 95% CI; 7. Plot MKI67 expression by T stage group with ER status stratification.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Regression summary table with estimate and p‐value for T stage; 2. Diagnostic plots of residuals; 3. Boxplot or violin plot of MKI67 expression by T stage and ER status."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Patients harboring TP53 somatic mutations show upregulation of cell cycle pathway genes compared to TP53 wild‐type patients.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess the impact of TP53 mutation status on expression of a curated cell cycle gene signature.",  
      "steps":"1. Load mutation and gene expression data; 2. Identify patients with any non‐silent TP53 mutation (IMPACT in {HIGH, MODERATE} and Variant_Classification ≠ 'Silent'); assign mutation_status='Mutated' or 'Wildtype'; 3. Define a cell cycle gene set (e.g., CCNA2, CCNB1, CDK1, CDK2, CDC20); 4. Compute per‐patient cell cycle score as the mean expression of these genes; 5. Merge mutation status and cell cycle score; 6. Compare scores between mutated and wild‐type using Wilcoxon test; 7. Visualize distributions with boxplot and jittered points.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Data frame of TP53 status and cell cycle score per patient; 2. Wilcoxon test statistic and p‐value; 3. Boxplot of cell cycle scores by TP53 status."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Tumor mutational burden positively correlates with an expression‐based immune infiltration score across the cohort.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify the relationship between somatic mutation count per patient and immune infiltration proxy derived from gene expression.",  
      "steps":"1. Load mutation, clinical, and gene expression data; 2. Compute TMB as total mutation count per patient; 3. Define immune infiltration signature genes (e.g., CD8A, GZMB, PRF1, CD3D, CD3E); 4. Calculate per‐patient immune score as mean expression of signature; 5. Merge TMB and immune score; 6. Compute Spearman correlation and test significance; 7. Plot scatter of immune score versus TMB with smoothing line.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of TMB and immune score per patient; 2. Spearman rho and p‐value; 3. Scatterplot with loess smoothing illustrating the relationship."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Hispanic or Latino patients exhibit a different distribution of somatic mutation types (Variant_Classification proportions) compared to non‐Hispanic patients.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test for differences in mutation classification profiles between ethnic groups.",  
      "steps":"1. Load clinical and mutation data; 2. Label patients as Hispanic/Latino vs Non‐Hispanic/Latino from ethnicity field; 3. Merge mutation records to assign ethnicity per record; 4. Tabulate counts of Variant_Classification (e.g., Missense, Nonsense, Frame_Shift, Silent) for each ethnic group; 5. Perform chi‐squared test of homogeneity on the contingency table; 6. If overall significant, compute standardized residuals to identify classifications driving differences; 7. Visualize with stacked bar chart of proportions by group.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Contingency table of classification counts; 2. Chi‐squared statistic, degrees of freedom, p‐value; 3. Bar chart of proportion of mutation classes by ethnicity; 4. Table of standardized residuals."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Radiation therapy administration is associated with altered expression of DNA damage response genes (e.g., ATM, BRCA1, RAD51), after controlling for pathologic stage.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare expression of key DNA repair genes between patients who received radiation and those who did not, adjusting for stage.",  
      "steps":"1. Load clinical and gene expression data; 2. Extract radiation_therapy (YES/NO) and pathologic_stage; drop missing; 3. Select DNA damage response genes: ATM, BRCA1, RAD51, CHEK2; 4. Compute per‐patient expression for each gene; 5. Fit multivariable linear models for each gene: expression ~ radiation + pathologic_stage (as factor); 6. Extract radiation coefficient and p‐value; apply multiple testing correction; 7. Summarize adjusted mean expressions by group and stage; plot grouped bar charts.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Regression summary with estimate and adjusted p‐value for radiation effect per gene; 2. Table of adjusted means by radiation status; 3. Bar plots with error bars of gene expression by group."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Higher HER2 IHC score (3+) is associated with increased ERBB2 expression and activation of its downstream signaling partners (e.g., GRB7, MYC) compared to HER2‐negative (0/1+).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Evaluate the relationship between HER2 immunohistochemistry score and expression of ERBB2 pathway genes.",  
      "steps":"1. Load clinical and gene expression data; 2. Extract her2_ihc_score, collapse into three categories: Negative (0/1+), Equivocal (2+), Positive (3+); 3. Select ERBB2, GRB7, MYC expression; 4. Merge score categories with expression; 5. Perform ANOVA for each gene across score groups; if assumptions fail, use Kruskal–Wallis; 6. Conduct post‐hoc pairwise comparisons with correction; 7. Plot gene expression boxplots colored by IHC score.",  
      "deliverables":"1. ANOVA (or KW) tables with p‐values; 2. Post‐hoc pairwise p‐values; 3. Boxplots of each gene’s expression by IHC score category."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Older patients at diagnosis have a higher ratio of silent to non‐silent somatic mutations, reflecting age‐related mutational processes.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Investigate association between patient age and the proportion of silent mutations among total mutations per patient.",  
      "steps":"1. Load clinical and mutation data; 2. Extract age_at_diagnosis; 3. For each patient, count silent and non‐silent mutations (Variant_Classification='Silent' vs others); 4. Compute silent_ratio = silent_count / total_mutations; 5. Merge ratio with age; 6. Compute Spearman correlation between age and silent_ratio; 7. Optionally fit beta regression (if ratio in (0,1)) adjusting for total TMB; 8. Plot silent_ratio versus age with smoothing line.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Data frame with age, silent_count, total, silent_ratio per patient; 2. Spearman rho and p‐value; 3. Beta regression coefficient and p‐value (if fitted); 4. Scatterplot of ratio vs age with trend line."  
    }  
  }  
]}
